{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"caption\":\"United States v. Whitaker\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2016-04-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"820 F.3d 849; Nos. 14-3290, 14-3506\",\"proposition\":\"A narcotics-dog sniff at an apartment door from a locked common hallway was a Fourth Amendment search under Katz and Kyllo because the dog detected otherwise unknowable details inside the home; the tenant’s inability to exclude ordinary hallway users did not eliminate privacy against sensitive investigative devices. This is the principal circuit-conflict anchor rejected by the Fourth Circuit in Johnson.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"canine sniffs; apartments; reasonable expectation of privacy; circuit split\",\"discovery_path\":\"Reintroduced as the contrary circuit rule in United States v. Johnson; primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca7/14-3290/14-3290-2016-04-12.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Leaders of a Beautiful Struggle v. Baltimore Police Department\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit (en banc)\",\"date\":\"2021-06-24\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"2 F.4th 330; No. 20-1495\",\"proposition\":\"Accessing Baltimore’s persistent, citywide aerial-surveillance archive was a search because the program allowed police to reconstruct the whole of individuals’ movements; its warrantless operation violated the Fourth Amendment under Carpenter’s aggregation rationale.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"aerial surveillance; persistent surveillance; mosaic theory; reasonable expectation of privacy\",\"discovery_path\":\"Identified as the principal pro-search aerial-surveillance authority distinguished in Hay and the recent pole-camera cases; primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/20-1495/20-1495-2021-06-24.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Tuggle\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2021-07-14\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"4 F.4th 505; No. 20-2352\",\"proposition\":\"Eighteen months of continuous warrantless pole-camera recording of publicly visible activity outside a home was not a search: officers used commonplace technology from lawful public locations to record what passersby could see. The court warned that ubiquitous, integrated camera networks could present a different question.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"pole cameras; prolonged visual surveillance; reasonable expectation of privacy; mosaic theory\",\"discovery_path\":\"Reaffirmed as controlling in House and analyzed in Hay and Moore-Bush; primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca7/20-2352/20-2352-2021-07-14.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Moore-Bush\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the First Circuit (en banc)\",\"date\":\"2022-06-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"36 F.4th 320; consolidated Nos. 19-1582, 19-1626, 19-1625, 19-1583\",\"proposition\":\"The en banc court unanimously reversed suppression but split evenly on whether eight months of searchable pole-camera surveillance of a home was a search: three judges applied Carpenter’s aggregation logic but found good-faith reliance on prior circuit law, while three concluded no search occurred. The First Circuit therefore remains without a majority rule on the search question.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"pole cameras; prolonged visual surveillance; mosaic theory; good faith; circuit split\",\"discovery_path\":\"Treated as the unresolved First Circuit position in Hay and House; primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca1/19-1582/19-1582-2022-06-09.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Christopher Perez\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2022-08-18\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"46 F.4th 691; No. 21-2130\",\"proposition\":\"The court avoided deciding whether a dog sniff at an interior apartment door violated Jardines and held suppression unavailable because officers reasonably relied on then-unoverruled Eighth Circuit precedent permitting such sniffs. Judge Kelly would have treated the door alcove as curtilage. Peck later repeated this unresolved posture.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"canine sniffs; apartment doors; curtilage; good faith\",\"discovery_path\":\"Revived as the controlling good-faith authority in Peck; primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca8/21-2130/21-2130-2022-08-18.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Ronquillo\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-03-07\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"94 F.4th 1169; No. 22-1247\",\"proposition\":\"A fenced, shielded detached garage about twenty-five feet from the residence, actually used for sleeping and other domestic activity, was curtilage under Dunn. The premises warrant covered it, and a detention there occurred within the search site’s immediate vicinity.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"curtilage; detached structures; warrant scope; search-site detention\",\"discovery_path\":\"Targeted curtilage search; published primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/22-1247/22-1247-2024-03-07.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Hay\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-03-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"95 F.4th 1304; No. 22-3276\",\"proposition\":\"Sixty-eight days of warrantless pole-camera surveillance—roughly fifteen hours daily—of activity naturally visible from the street outside a home, including incidental activity visible through a window, was not a search. Duration alone did not trigger Carpenter because the camera monitored one location rather than comprehensively tracking movements.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"pole cameras; prolonged visual surveillance; reasonable expectation of privacy; Carpenter limitation\",\"discovery_path\":\"Targeted pole-camera and aerial-surveillance-adjacent search; published primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/22-3276/22-3276-2024-03-19.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Frazer\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-04-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"98 F.4th 102; No. 23-4179\",\"proposition\":\"A fleeing suspect objectively abandoned a bag by throwing or placing it in a publicly accessible location more than forty feet away and continuing flight. Abandonment turns on objective words, acts, location, and circumstances known to officers, not an uncommunicated intent to reclaim the property.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"abandonment; discarded containers; flight; reasonable expectation of privacy\",\"discovery_path\":\"Targeted abandonment search; published primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/23-4179/23-4179-2024-04-09.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Green\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-02\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"106 F.4th 368; No. 19-4348\",\"proposition\":\"A regular non-overnight social guest with a deep familial relationship, years of visits, free rein of the home, and authority to bring friends had a reasonable expectation of privacy in the home and curtilage; bringing contraband did not forfeit it. Plain view authorized the gun’s seizure only after an arrest warrant independently supplied lawful access to the yard—plain view alone cannot authorize curtilage entry.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"reasonable expectation of privacy; social guests; curtilage; plain view; lawful access\",\"discovery_path\":\"Targeted REP, curtilage, and plain-view search; published primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/19-4348/19-4348-2024-07-02.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Coalition on Homelessness v. City and County of San Francisco\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-08\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"Published withdrawal order, No. 23-15087; withdrawing 90 F.4th 975\",\"proposition\":\"The panel withdrew both January 11, 2024 opinions, including the reported opinion at 90 F.4th 975, and replaced them with a nonprecedential memorandum. The replacement preserved a bag-and-tag injunction component only because the City had not challenged the preliminary Fourth Amendment relief; no Fourth Amendment or abandonment proposition from the withdrawn opinion remains precedential.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"tents and encampments; unattended property; abandonment; seizure; precedential status\",\"discovery_path\":\"Tent/encampment thread followed to the published withdrawal order: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/23-15087/23-15087-2024-07-08.html ; replacement memorandum: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/23-15087/23-15087-2024-07-08-0.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Chong v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-08-14\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"112 F.4th 848; No. 23-55140\",\"proposition\":\"An officer’s unconventional investigative entry to within one foot of a home was an unlicensed physical intrusion into curtilage and an obvious warrantless search under both the trespass and privacy tests. Good faith did not apply, and counsel was constitutionally ineffective for omitting the winning suppression argument; a mere visitor lacked a personal Fourth Amendment interest.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"curtilage; implied license; reasonable expectation of privacy; good faith; Sixth Amendment ineffective assistance\",\"discovery_path\":\"Targeted curtilage and Sixth Amendment search; published primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/23-55140/23-55140-2024-08-14.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. House\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-11-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-1950\",\"proposition\":\"The court expressly reaffirmed Tuggle: thirteen months of continuous warrantless pole-camera recording of publicly observable activity outside a home was not a search, and neither Carpenter nor the First Circuit’s divided Moore-Bush opinions justified revisiting that rule.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"pole cameras; prolonged visual surveillance; reasonable expectation of privacy; stare decisis\",\"discovery_path\":\"Targeted pole-camera search; primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca7/23-1950/23-1950-2024-11-05.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Mayberry\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-01-07\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"125 F.4th 132; No. 23-4051\",\"proposition\":\"A defendant abandoned a distinctive backpack by leaving it on a common hotel-stairwell landing and physically separating himself from it before arrest. Abandonment is an objective, fact-intensive inquiry, and the district court’s finding is reviewed for clear error; ownership alone does not preserve privacy after objective relinquishment.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"abandonment; containers; hotel common areas; reasonable expectation of privacy\",\"discovery_path\":\"Fourth Circuit abandonment line from Frazer through Lodge; official published opinion: https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/234051.P.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. McNeil\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-01-22\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-6923\",\"proposition\":\"After an unsuccessful front-door knock-and-talk—especially after children said the homeowner was absent—officers ordinarily may not roam into backyard curtilage without facts reasonably indicating the homeowner is elsewhere on the property. A suppression motion based on that entry had substantial potential merit, requiring an evidentiary hearing on counsel’s alleged Sixth Amendment ineffectiveness.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"curtilage; knock and talk; implied license; Sixth Amendment ineffective assistance\",\"discovery_path\":\"Targeted curtilage and ineffective-assistance search; published primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/22-6923/22-6923-2025-01-22.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Peck\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-03-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1198\",\"proposition\":\"The court again declined to decide whether a dog sniff at an interior apartment door is a search, holding suppression unavailable because officers objectively relied on existing Eighth Circuit precedent. The constitutional status of apartment-door sniffs therefore remains formally unresolved in the Eighth Circuit; Judge Kelly again would find protected curtilage and a violation.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"canine sniffs; apartment doors; curtilage; good faith; unresolved circuit law\",\"discovery_path\":\"Targeted canine-sniff search; published primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca8/24-1198/24-1198-2025-03-12.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Moses\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-07-03\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-3078\",\"proposition\":\"Ornelas abrogated circuit precedent treating curtilage as a factual issue reviewed only for clear error. Historical facts remain clear-error questions, but each Dunn factor’s legal significance and the ultimate curtilage conclusion receive de novo review. The exposed middle portion of the driveway was not curtilage, and the domestic-use factor turns on objective indications visible to a reasonable officer.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"curtilage; Dunn factors; standard of review; driveway; open fields boundary\",\"discovery_path\":\"Targeted curtilage search surfaced express abrogation of United States v. Benish; precedential opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca3/23-3078/23-3078-2025-07-03.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Johnson\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-08-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-4255; cert. denied, No. 25-774 (2026-04-20)\",\"proposition\":\"A drug-detection dog’s sniff immediately outside an apartment door in a common interior hallway was not a search. Under the privacy test, Place and Caballes controlled because the sniff disclosed only contraband; under the property test, the common hallway was not curtilage because the tenant lacked a right to exclude others. The court expressly rejected the Seventh Circuit’s Whitaker analysis, creating a direct circuit conflict.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"canine sniffs; apartments; curtilage; reasonable expectation of privacy; circuit split\",\"discovery_path\":\"Targeted canine-sniff search; official published opinion: https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/234255.P.pdf ; certiorari status: https://www.scotusblog.com/cases/johnson-v-united-states/\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Thompson v. Cockrell\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-09-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-2120\",\"proposition\":\"Officers received qualified immunity for a limited warrantless entry into fenced backyard curtilage to inspect a motorcycle VIN while performing a civil peacekeeping role. The court treated longstanding Eighth Circuit precedent permitting limited curtilage entry for a legitimate law-enforcement objective as still sufficient after Caniglia to prevent the asserted right from being clearly established.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"curtilage; limited-purpose entry; community caretaking; qualified immunity\",\"discovery_path\":\"Targeted curtilage search; primary opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca8/24-2120/24-2120-2025-09-17.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Watkins\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-05-11\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"156 F.4th 1049; No. 23-6210; panel opinion 2025-10-14, rehearing en banc denied 2026-05-11\",\"proposition\":\"The panel held that an officer conducted no search by standing on a publicly accessible exterior motel walkway and using unaided vision to peer through a one-inch curtain gap: the walkway was not curtilage and naturally visible interior details were not reasonably private from that vantage. Rehearing en banc was denied; two judges voted to rehear, and the dissent argued that privacy behind drawn curtains—including analogous tent flaps—should control.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"temporary dwellings; motel rooms; curtilage; plain view; reasonable expectation of privacy; en banc activity\",\"discovery_path\":\"Temporary-dwelling and plain-view thread; panel opinion and published rehearing order: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/23-6210/23-6210-2026-05-11.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Jones v. City of North Las Vegas\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-03-06\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-3374; original opinion 2025-09-08, amended opinion and rehearing denial 2026-03-06\",\"proposition\":\"Entering a small walled backyard was a presumptively unreasonable curtilage search, and hot pursuit did not justify it after officers lost track of the suspect for at least eighteen minutes and had not seen him enter that property. The right was clearly established, defeating qualified immunity. The amended opinion accompanied denial of rehearing en banc and an eight-judge dissent from that denial.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"curtilage; hot pursuit; exigent circumstances; qualified immunity; en banc activity\",\"discovery_path\":\"Targeted 2026 curtilage search; published amended opinion and rehearing order: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/24-3374/24-3374-2026-03-06.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Lodge\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-04-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-4665\",\"proposition\":\"A divided panel upheld the factual finding that a fleeing defendant abandoned a backpack after a resident rejected his attempt to place it inside a family-associated trailer and the bag was later found beside a backyard shed. Flight alone is not categorical abandonment, and the court expressly left unresolved whether hiding property within a familiar home’s curtilage ordinarily preserves privacy as concealment rather than abandonment.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"abandonment; curtilage; concealment; flight; reasonable expectation of privacy\",\"discovery_path\":\"Fourth Circuit abandonment thread following Frazer and Mayberry; official published opinion: https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/234665.P.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"}}
